ation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qiu M","lastName":"Qiu","firstName":"Mengyu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang K","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Kai","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhai J","lastName":"Zhai","firstName":"Jianjun","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng S","lastName":"Feng","firstName":"Shuying","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."},{"affiliation":"Henan Engineering Research Center for Chinese Medicine Foods for Special Medical Purpose, Zhengzhou, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4103371,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Bioeng Biotechnol","title":"Frontiers in bioengineering and biotechnology","essn":"2296-4185","issn":"2296-4185","isoabbreviation":"Front Bioeng Biotechnol","nlmid":"101632513"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1823483","abstractText":"Mesenchymal stem cell-derived exosomes (MSC-Exos) combined with CRISPR-Cas9 hold substantial therapeutic potential for immune-mediated diseases, although significant technical obstacles remain. This review presents a comprehensive analysis of strategies for engineering MSC-Exos as delivery vehicles for CRISPR-Cas9, with emphasis on cargo-loading methodologies and surface modifications that enable targeting specific immune cell populations. We examine the mechanistic basis for the therapeutic effects of these engineered platforms and critically assess the challenges impeding clinical translation, including manufacturing scalability, safety concerns, and regulatory considerations. Key areas of focus for this Research Topic-vehicle engineering, cargo packaging efficiency, biological barriers, and <i>in vivo</i> safety-are systematically addressed. Finally, we discuss emerging directions, including next-generation gene editors and stimulus-responsive biomaterials. This review provides a balanced framework for advancing MSC-Exos-based nanoplatforms toward precision gene therapies for immune disorders.","affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Immunotherapy","Autoimmune diseases","Targeted Delivery","Gene Editing","Clinical Translation","Crispr-cas9","Mesenchymal Stem Cell-derived Exosomes","Vehicle Engineering"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2026.1823483"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13527436"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13527436?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42787623","source":"MED","pmid":"42787623","pmcid":"PMC13601907","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13601907"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2026.1868779","title":"Clinical translation of CRISPR-Cas9 therapeutics in cancer and inherited genetic disorders.","authorString":"Bashir K, Vasudev P, Chhetri V, Dubhashi DP, Teli YG, Biswas R, Ramaiah S, Anbarasu A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bashir K","lastName":"Bashir","firstName":"Khushi","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Vasudev P","lastName":"Vasudev","firstName":"Prathiksha","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Chhetri V","lastName":"Chhetri","firstName":"Vikas","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubhashi DP","lastName":"Dubhashi","firstName":"Devika Prasad","initials":"DP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Teli YG","lastName":"Teli","firstName":"Yash Gurunath","initials":"YG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Biswas R","lastName":"Biswas","firstName":"Rhitam","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnology, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramaiah S","lastName":"Ramaiah","firstName":"Sudha","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Sciences, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Anbarasu A","lastName":"Anbarasu","firstName":"Anand","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnology, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","nlmid":"101775540"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1868779","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9, adapted from the bacterial Type II CRISPR adaptive immune system, functions as a programmable RNA-guided endonuclease that employs a single-guide RNA to direct Cas9 to specific genomic loci. CRISPR-Cas9 has transformed targeted genome editing by replacing complex protein engineering with programmable Watson-Crick base pairing between the guide RNA and target DNA. This review was developed following a structured literature search of major biomedical databases and clinical trial registries to synthesize current evidence on the therapeutic applications of CRISPR-Cas9 in oncology and inherited genetic disorders. Clinical studies of <i>ex vivo BCL11A</i>-enhancer editing have shown fetal hemoglobin reactivation, with most evaluable participants with sickle cell disease remaining free of severe vaso-occlusive crises for the prespecified period and most evaluable participants with transfusion-dependent β-thalassemia achieving sustained transfusion independence. <i>In vivo</i> reductions in circulating transthyretin protein levels have been achieved for transthyretin amyloidosis via lipid nanoparticle delivery, while clinically meaningful improvements in selected measures of visual function were observed in a subset of patients receiving subretinal AAV-delivered CRISPR editing for <i>CEP290</i>-associated Leber congenital amaurosis type 10. Preclinical and early clinical studies have further investigated CRISPR-engineered T cells designed to improve antitumor activity, persistence, or resistance to inhibitory signaling. Despite these advances, key translational hurdles include the risk of off-target mutations and large-scale chromosomal rearrangements. Furthermore, immune responses against bacterial Cas9 nucleases and viral delivery vectors may limit the long-term efficacy of CRISPR-based therapies, while technical barriers surrounding delivery to extrahepatic tissues, such as skeletal muscle and the central nervous system, continue to hinder broader clinical success. Ethical concerns regarding germline modifications and the high cost of individualized therapies present additional translational challenges. Consequently, emerging DSB-independent technologies, such as base editing and prime editing, may reduce selected DSB-associated liabilities, but each introduces distinct editing, delivery, and genotoxicity risks that require product-specific evaluation.","affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Gene therapy","Cancer","Genome Editing","Clinical Translation","Crispr-cas9","Inherited Genetic Disorders"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1868779"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601907"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601907?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42539480","source":"MED","pmid":"42539480","pmcid":"PMC13423668","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13423668"]},"doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1888734","title":"CRISPR/Cas9 in cancer therapy: clinical translation, mechanistic strategies, and therapeutic directions.","authorString":"Ng CX, Mustafa S, Yap XY, Lee SH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ng CX","lastName":"Ng","firstName":"Chu Xin","initials":"CX","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa S","lastName":"Mustafa","firstName":"Sakina","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Yap XY","lastName":"Yap","firstName":"Xin Yi","initials":"XY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee SH","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Sau Har","initials":"SH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."},{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Medical Advancements Impact Lab, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Active Living, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":4110619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oncol","title":"Frontiers in oncology","essn":"2234-943X","issn":"2234-943X","isoabbreviation":"Front Oncol","nlmid":"101568867"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1888734","abstractText":"The advent of CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing has significantly transformed the landscape of cancer therapeutics by facilitating precise and programmable manipulation of disease-associated genetic modifications. This review comprehensively evaluates the current clinical and translational landscape of CRISPR/Cas9-based cancer therapies through an analysis of published literature and registered clinical trials. The current CRISPR/Cas9 applications in oncology are primarily centred on three mechanistic strategies: immune cell engineering for enhanced tumor recognition, direct targeting of oncogenic mutations, and modulation of tumor-supportive pathways. Analysis of 32 clinical trials indicates that CRISPR-based interventions have demonstrated encouraging safety profiles and early signs of clinical activity, particularly in <i>ex vivo</i> engineered immune-cell therapies. Notable examples include CRISPR-edited CAR-T cell products targeting <i>CD19</i> and <i>BCMA</i>, which have achieved objective responses in relapsed or refractory hematological malignancies while demonstrating sustained persistence of edited cells <i>in vivo</i>. In contrast, clinical translation into solid tumors remains comparatively limited due to challenges associated with delivery efficiency, tumor heterogeneity, and the immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment. Technological advancements, including multiplex genome editing, base editing, and prime editing have expanded the precision and versatility of CRISPR-based interventions, while integration with immunotherapy and nanotechnology-based delivery systems continues to broaden therapeutic potential. Despite these advances, several significant challenges still need to be addressed, including off-target editing, manufacturing scalability, delivery limitations, and regulatory considerations. Overall, CRISPR/Cas9 represents a promising yet evolving platform in oncology, with its future clinical success dependent on achieving a balance between precision, safety, scalability, and long-term therapeutic durability.","affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cancer therapy","Clinical Trials","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Precision Oncology","Car-t Cell Therapy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fonc.2026.1888734"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13423668"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13423668?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42819672","source":"MED","pmid":"42819672","pmcid":"PMC13624556","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13624556"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2026.1888079","title":"Overcoming delivery barriers of large all-in-one CRISPR/Cas9 plasmids using lysine-based lipid nanoparticles.","authorString":"Sung W, Li T, Takeoka S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sung W","lastName":"Sung","firstName":"Wenhan","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li T","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Tianshu","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liberal Arts and Sciences Education Center, Musashi University, Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Advanced Research of Biosystem Dynamics, Research Institute for Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takeoka S","lastName":"Takeoka","firstName":"Shinji","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Advanced Research of Biosystem Dynamics, Research Institute for Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","nlmid":"101775540"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1888079","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) are widely used as drug delivery systems (DDS) for the intracellular delivery of gene-editing tools such as the clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (Cas9) system. Previously, a lysine-headgroup cationic lipid (K3C16) was reported to exhibit high biocompatibility and cellular internalization via plasma membrane fusion when formulated as liposomes, suggesting its potential as a low-toxicity gene delivery material. However, the translation of this lipid into a multi-component lipid nanoparticle (LNP) matrix via microfluidic engineering for large cargo encapsulation has not been explored. In this study, K3C16 was successfully engineered for the first time as the main lipid component of LNPs to deliver large plasmid DNA encoding the CRISPR/Cas9 system.<h4>Methods</h4>First, 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine (DOPE) and 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine (DOPC) were evaluated as helper phospholipids to identify an optimal lipid composition. LNPs containing DOPE exhibited significantly higher transfection efficiency than those containing DOPC and were therefore selected for subsequent experiments. Next, LNPs formulated with different cationic or ionizable lipids were prepared and evaluated in HEK293 cells for transfection efficiency, cytotoxicity, and gene-editing efficiency.<h4>Results</h4>LNPs containing the commercial ionizable lipid SM-102 showed higher transfection and gene-editing efficiencies than the lysine-headgroup cationic lipids represent a promising and biocompatible platform for CRISPR/Cas9 plasmid delivery; however, increased cytotoxicity was observed in highly transfected cell populations. In contrast, the lysine-headgroup cationic LMPs achieved effective delivery of large plasmid DNA and CRISPR/Cas9-mediated gene editing while maintaining superior biocompatibility.<h4>Conclusion</h4>These results demonstrate that lysine-headgroup cationic lipids represent a promising and biocompatible platform for CRISPR/Cas9 plasmid delivery. Further optimization of lipid composition and molar ratios may enhance transfection and gene-editing efficiencies, advancing the development of safer nonviral gene delivery systems.","affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biocompatibility","Non-viral Gene Delivery","Cationic Lipids","Crispr/cas9","Lipid Nanoparticles (Lnps)","Aminolipids"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1888079"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13624556"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13624556?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42511812","source":"MED","pmid":"42511812","pmcid":"PMC13411468","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13411468"]},"doi":"10.3390/ijms27146469","title":"Application of CRISPR-Cas9-Based Gene Editing Technology in Inherited Liver Diseases.","authorString":"Liu R, Cong S, Gao Y, Xu J, Shi X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu R","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Ran","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Laboratory of Biotechnology and Molecular Drug Research and Development, School of Life Sciences, Liaoning Normal University, Dalian 116082, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cong S","lastName":"Cong","firstName":"Shiqi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Laboratory of Biotechnology and Molecular Drug Research and Development, School of Life Sciences, Liaoning Normal University, Dalian 116082, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gao Y","lastName":"Gao","firstName":"Yuan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Laboratory of Biotechnology and Molecular Drug Research and Development, School of Life Sciences, Liaoning Normal University, Dalian 116082, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu J","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Jiaqi","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Laboratory of Biotechnology and Molecular Drug Research and Development, School of Life Sciences, Liaoning Normal University, Dalian 116082, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi X","lastName":"Shi","firstName":"Xiaoxia","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1753-241X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Laboratory of Biotechnology and Molecular Drug Research and Development, School of Life Sciences, Liaoning Normal University, Dalian 116082, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1753-241X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"14","volume":"27","journalIssueId":4228484,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci","title":"International journal of molecular sciences","essn":"1422-0067","issn":"1422-0067","isoabbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci","nlmid":"101092791"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"6469","abstractText":"Inherited liver diseases are predominantly caused by monogenic mutations, and the vast majority of these conditions currently lack curative treatment options. Although liver transplantation may be used for patients with end-stage disease, it faces numerous challenges, including donor organ shortage, immune rejection, and the need for lifelong immunosuppression. In recent years, CRISPR-Cas9-based gene editing technology has advanced rapidly, offering transformative hope for the treatment of these diseases. This review systematically elucidates the working principles and technical advantages of the CRISPR-Cas9 system and its derived tools (base editing and prime editing), summarizes recent applications of these technologies in the treatment of hereditary liver diseases, and discusses the prospects and challenges of their clinical translation, aiming to provide a theoretical reference for future research in this field.","affiliation":"Liaoning Provincial Key Labo