Man":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42510829","source":"MED","pmid":"42510829","pmcid":"PMC13409911","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13409911"]},"doi":"10.3390/genes17070789","title":"MEK1 as a Synthetic Lethal Target with Cabozantinib in Renal Cell Carcinoma: Insights from CRISPR/Cas9 Screening.","authorString":"Yoshino H, Fukuda I, Enokida H, Seki N, Goto Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yoshino H","lastName":"Yoshino","firstName":"Hirofumi","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5470-7445"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Graduate School of Medical and Dental Sciences, Kagoshima University, Kagoshima 890-8520, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Fukuda I","lastName":"Fukuda","firstName":"Ikumi","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Graduate School of Medical and Dental Sciences, Kagoshima University, Kagoshima 890-8520, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Enokida H","lastName":"Enokida","firstName":"Hideki","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Graduate School of Medical and Dental Sciences, Kagoshima University, Kagoshima 890-8520, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Seki N","lastName":"Seki","firstName":"Naohiko","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4731-7956"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Genomics, Chiba University Graduate School of Medicine, Chiba 260-8670, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Goto Y","lastName":"Goto","firstName":"Yusuke","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7720-9740"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Genomics, Chiba University Graduate School of Medicine, Chiba 260-8670, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Teikyo University Chiba Medical Centre, Chiba 299-0111, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7720-9740"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5470-7445"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4731-7956"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4234403,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Genes (Basel)","title":"Genes","essn":"2073-4425","issn":"2073-4425","isoabbreviation":"Genes (Basel)","nlmid":"101551097"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"789","abstractText":"<b>Background/Objectives</b>: Cabozantinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that primarily targets MET. It has become an important drug in the treatment of renal cell carcinoma (RCC); however, many patients do not respond to cabozantinib treatment and there is no effective next-line therapy. In this study, we identified molecular-targeted drugs that exhibit synergistic effects with cabozantinib using CRISPR/Cas9 screening. <b>Methods</b>: A kinome-wide synthetic lethal CRISPR/Cas9 screen was used to identify target molecules using 786-o RCC cells. A library was generated, and treatment with vehicle or cabozantinib was carried out, followed by next-generation sequencing to identify candidate genes. A combination index based on the Chou-Talalay method was used to evaluate the synergistic effect of cabozantinib through cell viability assays. Xenograft assays were conducted to determine the effect in vivo. <b>Results</b>: CRISPR/Cas9-based screening revealed four genes (<i>MEK1</i>, <i>DCLK1</i>, <i>DYRK3</i>, and <i>FGFR1</i>) that were candidates for synthetic lethality by cabozantinib in RCC cells. We focused on MEK1 because the MEK1 inhibitor cobimetinib has been approved for melanoma treatment. In a cell proliferation assay using 786-o and A498 RCC cells, the combination of cobimetinib and cabozantinib exhibited a synergistic effect. A xenograft assay also revealed a significant synergistic effect of cobimetinib and cabozantinib. <b>Conclusions</b>: CRISPR/Cas9 screening identified MEK1 as a candidate for a synthetic lethal target with cabozantinib in RCC. The combined inhibition of MET/VEGFR and MEK1 suppressed compensatory MAPK reactivation and downregulated the PI3K-Akt pathway, including the survival-associated genes PPP2R3B and ATF6B, and produced significant tumor growth suppression in vivo. These findings highlight the potential of cabozantinib plus cobimetinib, an already-FDA-approved MEK inhibitor, as a readily translatable combination strategy to overcome cabozantinib resistance in RCC.","affiliation":"Department of Urology, Graduate School of Medical and Dental Sciences, Kagoshima University, Kagoshima 890-8520, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"24K11347","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"24K12458","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"24K12641","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Carcinoma, Renal Cell","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":