sion":"2026-09-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42691507","source":"MED","pmid":"42691507","pmcid":"PMC13679631","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13679631"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.tranon.2026.102999","title":"In vivo and FACS-based CRISPR/Cas9 screening uncovers VENTX as a crucial element in maintaining seminoma stemness.","authorString":"Huang C, Chen Y, Zhang E, Wang Y, Liu Y, Tang L, Chen C, Lin P, Wang B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang C","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Chaoxiong","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen Y","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Yiran","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang E","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Enhuan","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anxi County Hospital, Quanzhou, 362400, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Yifan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Yixuan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang L","lastName":"Tang","firstName":"Lirui","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen C","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Chuanben","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China. Electronic address: ccb@fjmu.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin P","lastName":"Lin","firstName":"Peicheng","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China. Electronic address: L13599439977@163.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang B","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Bingyi","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China. Electronic address: wangby003@126.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"73","journalIssueId":4290815,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Transl Oncol","title":"Translational oncology","essn":"1936-5233","issn":"1936-5233","isoabbreviation":"Transl Oncol","nlmid":"101472619"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"102999","abstractText":"Seminoma, which is the most frequent testicular germ cell tumor, has stem cell-like features related to treatment resistance and recurrence, yet the molecular mechanisms that preserve its stemness are not well understood. This study aimed to discover the tumorigenesis and stemness regulation of seminoma. In vivo CRISPR/Cas9 knockout library screening was performed using Tcam-2 seminoma cells implanted into NSG mice to identify tumorigenesis drivers. Parallel FACS-based screening enriched CD117/CD133 double-negative cells to assess the regulators of seminoma stemness. Functional validation included clonogenicity assays, sphere formation tests, and tumor initiation experiments in vivo. RNA sequencing analyzed downstream pathways. In vivo and FACS-based CRISPR/Cas9 screening uncovers VENTX as a crucial element in maintaining seminoma stemness. Functional validation showed that VENTX knockout decreased clonogenicity, sphere formation, and CD117+/CD133+ populations in Tcam-2 cells, and also hindered tumor initiation in vivo. RNA sequencing identified a connection between VENTX and pluripotency pathways, demonstrating a downregulation of ID1/2/3 (essential differentiation inhibitors) and an imbalance in Wnt/β-catenin and TGF-β signaling. VENTX expression showed a significant association with DNA methylation-based stemness scores in testicular germ cell tumors. In conclusion, VENTX is a key regulator of seminoma stemness, presenting a promising target for therapy to address recurrence and resistance in seminoma.","affiliation":"Clinical Oncology School of Fujian Medical University, Fujian Cancer Hospital, Fuzhou, 350014, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nanog","Seminoma","Stemness","Ventx","Crispr/cas9 Library Screening"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tranon.2026.102999"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13679631"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13679631?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-21","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42412819","source":"MED","pmid":"42412819","pmcid":"PMC13340169","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13340169"]},"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btag210","title":"CROP: a feature-independent context-aware method for CRISPR-Cas9 frameshift prediction.","authorString":"Tziony I, Orenstein Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tziony I","lastName":"Tziony","firstName":"Ido","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-5738-0882"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat Gan 5290002, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Orenstein Y","lastName":"Orenstein","firstName":"Yaron","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3583-3112"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat Gan 5290002, Israel."},{"affiliation":"The Mina and Everard Goodman Faculty of Life Sciences, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat Gan 5290002, Israel."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3583-3112"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-5738-0882"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"42","journalIssueId":4259227,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bioinformatics","title":"Bioinformatics (Oxford, England)","essn":"1367-4811","issn":"1367-4803","isoabbreviation":"Bioinformatics","nlmid":"9808944"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"btag210","abstractText":"<h4>Motivation</h4>The CRISPR-Cas9 complex has revolutionized genome-editing technologies. By designing a 20 nt-long guide RNA, a Cas9 nuclease can be guided to cleave almost any genomic target site (followed by NGG). The cleavage induces double-stranded DNA breaks, which are then repaired by cellular pathways. Accurate CRISPR-Cas9 repair-outcome prediction is essential for designing guide RNAs with desired genomic effects, such as gene knockout. A central challenge is quantifying the rate of frameshifts, i.e. repair-outcomes that lead to a change in the local length that is not a multiple of three. Previous methods for frameshift-rate prediction were trained on only a few experimental or cellular contexts, mostly relied on manually defined microhomology features, and were limited by sparse features and class labels.<h4>Results</h4>We developed CROP, a feature-independent context-aware repair-outcome prediction method. By aggregating specific repair outcomes as Δlength classes, CROP overcomes class sparsity. We designed CROP to work with variable input sequence lengths and output classes to utilize multiple datasets simultaneously. We benchmarked CROP against state-of-the-art repair-outcome prediction methods over 18 datasets, which we curated and standardized from various studies. Across all datasets, CROP outperformed all competing methods in frameshift-rate prediction. We performed cross-experiment and cross-cellular frameshift-rate predictions to investigate the generalizability of repair mechanisms. Finally, we show that CROP learned microhomology principles from raw sequences without explicit feature engineering, establishing an end-to-end architecture for CRISPR-Cas9 repair-outcome prediction that learns from multiple datasets.<h4>Availability and implementation</h4>CROP is available at https://github.com/OrensteinLab/CROP.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat Gan 5290002, Israel.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Israel Science Foundation","grantId":"358/21","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computational Biology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Frameshift Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Software"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prediction Algorithms"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btag210"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13340169"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13340169?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"PPR","id":"PPR1227550","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-08","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42371233","source":"MED","pmid":"42371233","pmcid":"PMC13538318","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13538318"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12284-026-00923-7","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-Mediated Mutagenesis of OsERF94 Enhances Pre-Harvest Sprouting in Rice.","authorString":"Lee MB, Lee HN, Chu SH, Park YJ, Park CW, Lee IJ, Cho LH, Kim JY.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lee MB","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Man Bo","initials":"MB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee HN","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Ha Neul","initials":"HN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Chu SH","lastName":"Chu","firstName":"Sang-Ho","initials":"SH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Park YJ","lastName":"Park","firstName":"Yong-Jin","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Park CW","lastName":"Park","firstName":"Chang-Wook","initials":"CW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Biosciences, Kyungpook National University, Daegu, 41566, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee IJ","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"In-Jung","initials":"IJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Biosciences, Kyungpook National University, Daegu, 41566, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Cho LH","lastName":"Cho","firstName":"Lae-Hyeon","initials":"LH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Bioscience, College of Natural Resources and Life Science, Pusan National University, Miryang, 50463, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim JY","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Jae Yoon","initials":"JY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea. jaeyoonkim@kongju.ac.kr."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4209463,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Rice (N Y)","title":"Rice (New York, N.Y.)","essn":"1939-8433","issn":"1939-8425","isoabbreviation":"Rice (N Y)","nlmid":"101503136"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"58","abstractText":"Pre-harvest sprouting (PHS), where seeds germinate on panicles before harvest under humid conditions, is a serious global issue in cereal crop production, including rice. Fine-mapping of the previously reported chromosome 4 locus identified OsERF94 as a strong candidate gene for functional validation. In this study, we investigated the role of OsERF94 in PHS using CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing. The CRISPR/Cas9-mediated mutagenesis of OsERF94 induced frameshift mutations, resulting in a loss-of-function of OsERF94 in the 1-I-ET and 2-D-ET lines. The 1-I-ET and 2-D-ET lines exhibited significantly higher germination rates under PHS conditions compared to the wild type, indicating increased susceptibility to PHS. Whole-genome re-sequencing confirmed that few or no mutations could be detected at off-target candidate sites in both edited lines, ensuring the precision of the CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing. A transcriptome analysis revealed altered expression patterns of several GA-related genes, including OsLOL1, OsKO3, OsGA3ox2, and OsGA2ox5 in the OsERF94 mutant lines. The up-regulation of GA biosynthetic genes and the down-regulation of GA deactivation genes observed in both the OsERF94 mutant lines suggest possible alterations in GA metabolism during the early stages of PHS. Transient luciferase reporter assays using a single-luciferase system suggested that OsERF94 may be associated with changes in the promoter activities of several GA- and ethylene-related genes. These findings suggest that OsERF94 may contribute to the regulation of PHS, potentially through moderation of GA- and ethylene-related pathways. Overall, this study improves our understanding of the molecular role of OsERF94 in PHS and highlights its potential as a target for the genetic improvement of PHS resistance in rice-breeding programs.","affiliation":"Department of Plant Resources, College of Industrial Science, Kongju National University, Yesan, 32439, South Korea.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"R&amp;D program for Rural Development Administration","grantId":"RS-2024-00322431","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant funded by the Korean government","grantId":"2022R1A4A1030348","orderIn":0},{"agency":"R&D program for Rural Development Administration (RDA)","grantId":"RS-2024-00322431","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant funded by the Korean government (MSIT)","grantId":"2022R1A4A1030348","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Germination","Pre-harvest Sprouting","Crispr/cas9","Ga Biosynthesis","Oserf94","Ga Deactivation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12284-026-00923-7"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13538318"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13538318?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42427441","source":"MED","pmid":"42427441","pmcid":"PMC13347127","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13347127"]},"doi":"10.21769/bioprotoc.5734","title":"A Dual-gRNA CRISPR/Cas9 System for Efficient Generation of Large Fragment Deletions in Poplar.","authorString":"Yang G, Yu Y, Vulavala VKR, Dwivedi N, Liu CJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang G","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Guoqian","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu Y","lastName":"Yu","firstName":"Yang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA."},{"affiliation":"Jiangsu Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Nanjing, Jiangsu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Vulavala VKR","lastName":"Vulavala","firstName":"Vijaya Kumar Reddy","initials":"VKR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dwivedi N","lastName":"Dwivedi","firstName":"Nidhi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu CJ","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Chang-Jun","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"13","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4229181,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bio Protoc","title":"Bio-protocol","essn":"2331-8325","issn":"2331-8325","isoabbreviation":"Bio Protoc","nlmid":"101635102"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e5734","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9-based genome editing is a powerful approach for functional genomics and bioenergy research in woody plants. However, conventional single guide RNA (gRNA) strategies predominantly generate small insertions or deletions that may not fully disrupt gene function and often require extensive sequencing for mutation identification. Here, we present an optimized protocol for the efficient generation of large-fragment deletion mutants in <i>Populus tremula × P. alba</i> clone INRA 717-1B4 using a dual-gRNA CRISPR/Cas9 system. Co-expression of two gRNAs flanking the target region induces double-strand breaks at both sites, enabling the deletion of the intervening genomic fragment, typically larger than 50 bp. This protocol describes step-by-step procedures for gRNA design, vector construction, <i>Agrobacterium</i>-mediated transformation, plant regeneration, and molecular validation. Using the <i>PtFBX230</i> gene as a representative target, large deletions are readily identified by conventional PCR and agarose gel electrophoresis, enabling rapid and cost-effective genotyping. This protocol can be readily adopted to other loci in poplar and related woody species and provides a robust framework for generating null alleles to support functional genomics and bioenergy-related trait engineering in woody plants. Key features • Enables efficient deletion of large genomic fragments (>50 bp) using a dual-gRNA CRISPR/Cas9 strategy. • Optimized for the hybrid poplar (<i>Populus tremula × P. alba</i> clone INRA 717-1B4) using the pORE303N vector system. • Compatible with <i>Agrobacterium</i>-mediated transformation and tissue culture-based regeneration. • Simplifies mutant screening through PCR-based genotyping, minimizing reliance on sequencing. • Facilitates simultaneous editing of homologous genes in bioenergy-relevant poplar lines.","affiliation":"Biology Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory, Upton, NY, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["methods-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Genome Editing","Bioenergy Crop","Crispr/cas9","Dual-grna Strategy","Populus Tremula × P. Alba Inra 717-1B4","Ptfbx230"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21769/BioProtoc.5734"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13347127"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13347127?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42031935","source":"MED","pmid":"42031935","pmcid":"PMC13276357","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13276357"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-49093-y","title":"A method for CRISPR/Cas9-induced genetic barcoding and lineage tracing in sheep.","authorString":"Hao D, Xu X, Li P, Liu Y, Liu G, Deng S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hao D","lastName":"Hao","firstName":"Dayong","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Reproduction Center, The Third Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou, 450052, China."},{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Animal Biotech Breeding, Beijing Key Laboratory for Animal Genetic Improvement, National Engineering Laboratory for Animal Breeding, Key Laboratory of Animal Genetics and Breeding of the Ministry of Agriculture, Frontiers Science Center for Molecular Design Breeding (MOE), College of Animal Science and Technology, China Agricultural University, Beijing, 100193, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu X","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Xueling","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Bee Science and Biomedicine, Fujian Agriculture and Forestry University, Fuzhou, 350002, China."},{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Animal Biotech Breeding, Beijing Key Laboratory for Animal Genetic Improvement, National Engineering Laboratory for Animal Breeding, Key Laboratory of Animal Genetics and Breeding of the Ministry of Agriculture, Frontiers Science Center for Molecular Design Breeding (MOE), College of Animal Science and Technology, China Agricultural University, Beijing, 100193, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li P","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Pan","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Xianghu Laboratory, Hangzhou, 311231, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Yanli","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Reproduction Center, The Third Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou, 450052, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu G","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Guoshi","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Animal Biotech Breeding, Beijing Key Laboratory for Animal Genetic Improvement, National Engineering Laboratory for Animal Breeding, Key Laboratory of Animal Genetics and Breeding of the Ministry of Agriculture, Frontiers Science Center for Molecular Design Breeding (MOE), College of Animal Science and Technology, China Agricultural University, Beijing, 100193, China. gshliu@cau.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Deng S","lastName":"Deng","firstName":"Shoulong","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Science Center for Model Animals, China Agricultural University, Beijing, 100193, China. dengsl@cau.edu.cn."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4168316,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","title":"Scientific reports","essn":"2045-2322","issn":"2045-2322","isoabbreviation":"Sci Rep","nlmid":"101563288"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"18886","abstractText":"Sheep development involves continuous dynamic processes in which cells propagate, differentiate and orchestrate. However, the method for tracing cell fate during sheep (Ovis aries) embryogenesis, cell differentiation and tissue regeneration remains largely undeveloped. Here, we developed a CRISPR/Cas9-based lineage barcode recording method that directly acts on target cells. With this method, several contiguous CRISPR/Cas9 targeting arrays were synthesized and introduced into multiple loci in the sheep genome using the PiggyBac transposon vector to form the barcode region. Cas9 integrated at the Rosa26 gene locus was used to generate edits in the barcode region at multiple timepoints during early sheep embryonic development. We detected multiple integrated barcodes (intBCs) that were stably inherited in the developing embryos, confirming that the method can generate heritable clonal markers. The method could enable lineage tracing in sheep when combined with single-cell sequencing technologies. Our method establishes a foundation for ruminant lineage tracing technology by combining PiggyBac transposons and CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing tools, providing a new platform for analyzing sheep embryonic development, organ regeneration, and disease mechanisms.","affiliation":"The Reproduction Center, The Third Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou, 450052, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Non-profit Central Research Institute Fund of Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences","grantId":"2023-PT180-01","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sheep","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EM","qualifierName":"embryology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA Transposable Elements","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cell Lineage","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Embryonic Development","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"DNA Barcoding, Taxonomic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifie