r Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Jameel A","lastName":"Jameel","firstName":"Aysha","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Horváth GV","lastName":"Horváth","firstName":"Gábor V","initials":"GV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Török K","lastName":"Török","firstName":"Katalin","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Ayaydin F","lastName":"Ayaydin","firstName":"Ferhan","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."},{"affiliation":"Functional Cell Biology and Immunology Advanced Core Facility (FCBI-ACF), Hungarian Centre of Excellence for Molecular Medicine (HCEMM), Szeged, Hungary."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, Albert Szent-Györgyi Health Centre, Interdisciplinary R&D and Innovation Centre of Excellence, University of Szeged, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"László N","lastName":"László","firstName":"Nikolett","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."},{"affiliation":"Doctoral School of Biology, University of Szeged, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Dondapati DT","lastName":"Dondapati","firstName":"Divya Teja","initials":"DT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Functional Cell Biology and Immunology Advanced Core Facility (FCBI-ACF), Hungarian Centre of Excellence for Molecular Medicine (HCEMM), Szeged, Hungary."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, Albert Szent-Györgyi Health Centre, Interdisciplinary R&D and Innovation Centre of Excellence, University of Szeged, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Mórocz S","lastName":"Mórocz","firstName":"Sándor","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."},{"affiliation":"Cereal Research Non-profit Ltd., Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Zombori Z","lastName":"Zombori","firstName":"Zoltán","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Ferenc G","lastName":"Ferenc","firstName":"Györgyi","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}},{"fullName":"Dudits D","lastName":"Dudits","firstName":"Dénes","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","nlmid":"101775540"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1924094","abstractText":"Given the growing emphasis on the efficient application of CRISPR/Cas9 in plant genome editing, there is a need for methodologies that enhance targeted mutagenesis efficiency. In the present study, we utilized stable transgenic maize callus tissues harboring active CRISPR/Cas9 vector systems targeting either the <i>abnormal cytokinin response 1</i> (<i>ARE1</i>) gene or the <i>potassium channel</i> gene (<i>KAT2</i>). The presented DNA sequencing data indicate that the <i>KAT2</i>-targeting vector, which utilizes a 3'-end truncated guide RNA, retained mutagenic activity, albeit at a notably low frequency. We quantified mutagenic events following exposure to elevated temperatures (37 °C for 48 h) and various chromatin-modifying compounds, including sodium butyrate (NaBt), nicotinamide (NA), and 5-azacytidine (AZA). High-temperature treatment alone increased the frequency of nucleotide deletions (5.8-fold relative to the untreated samples) in the <i>ARE1</i> target gene. Conversely, in the <i>KAT2</i> gene, both deletions and insertions were stimulated (6.4 and 6.2-fold, respectively). As a novel approach, we evaluated the mutagenic efficacy of chromatin-modifying compounds administered in conjunction with heat treatment. The combined application of NaBt and AZA with elevated temperature was particularly effective in increasing insertion frequencies; in the <i>ARE1</i> (with NaBt + heat: 17.3-fold) and <i>KAT2</i> (with AZA + heat: 21.4-fold) genes. Cell cycle analysis of suspension-cultured cells revealed that chromatin-modifying treatments altered cell cycle progression, evidenced by an accumulation of cells in the G2 phase and a significant reduction in the number of mitotic cells. Subsequent DNA sequencing demonstrated a differential increase in the induction of deletions and insertions, alongside an expansion of the deleted regions.","affiliation":"Agribiotechnology and Precision Breeding for Food Security National Laboratory, Institute of Plant Biology, HUN-REN Biological Research Centre, Szeged, Hungary.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nicotinamide","Maize","Heat treatment","Sodium butyrate","5-azacytidine","Crispr/cas9","Gene Editing Efficiency"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1924094"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13683662"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13683662?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41674084","source":"MED","pmid":"41674084","pmcid":"PMC12895976","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12895976"]},"doi":"10.1002/tpg2.70177","title":"Optimization of CRISPR/Cas9-mediated CtPDS knockout in guar protoplasts.","authorString":"Ghosh PK, Biswas S, Malaker R, Pham H, Septiningsih EM, Ravelombola W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ghosh PK","lastName":"Ghosh","firstName":"Protik Kumar","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8349-571X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA."},{"affiliation":"Texas A&M AgriLife Research-Vernon, Vernon, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Biswas S","lastName":"Biswas","firstName":"Sudip","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8062-2149"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Malaker R","lastName":"Malaker","firstName":"Roly","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0427-2647"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pham H","lastName":"Pham","firstName":"Hanh","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Texas A&M AgriLife Research-Lubbock, Lubbock, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Septiningsih EM","lastName":"Septiningsih","firstName":"Endang M","initials":"EM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9481-304X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ravelombola W","lastName":"Ravelombola","firstName":"Waltram","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1879-9872"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA."},{"affiliation":"Texas A&M AgriLife Research-Vernon, Vernon, Texas, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8062-2149"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1879-9872"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8349-571X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9481-304X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0427-2647"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4080392,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Plant Genome","title":"The plant genome","essn":"1940-3372","issn":"1940-3372","isoabbreviation":"Plant Genome","nlmid":"101273919"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70177","abstractText":"Guar (Cyamopsis tetragonoloba L. Taub.) is a climate-resilient legume with industrial and agricultural applications. Recently, gene editing has emerged as a key genetic tool for crop improvement. Despite its recent increasing value as a commodity for various uses, there is no documented report of gene editing work in guar to date. In this study, we present the first optimized protocol for protoplast-based clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (CRISPR/Cas9) genome editing in guar. The most intact and viable protoplasts were observed in the cotyledons of 6-day-old seedlings that were isolated using the tape-sandwich method. Enzymatic digestion with 1.5% cellulase RS and 0.3% pectinase in 600 mM mannitol, followed by 10 min of vacuum infiltration, increased protoplast release and viability. Polyethylene glycol (PEG)-mediated transformation had been tailored using 40% PEG for 5 min with green fluorescent protein plasmid, and the cestrum yellow leaf curling virus promoter at room temperature showed the highest transient expression efficiency (∼57%). A multiplexed CRISPR/Cas9 construct was designed to target the Cyamopsis tetragonoloba phytoene desaturase (CtPDS) gene in guar. Polymerase chain reaction amplification and Sanger sequencing of transfected protoplasts confirmed highly efficient editing, with fragment deletions ranging from 714 to 1061 bp in CtPDS. Overall, we achieved 100% editing efficiency, as all successfully transformed samples showed CRISPR/Cas9-induced mutations. These findings establish a reliable, transient protoplast system for functional genomics and targeted trait improvement in guar, providing a key foundation for future crop improvement.","affiliation":"Department of Soil and Crop Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, Texas, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"USDA-ARS Germplasm Evaluation","grantId":"58-6046-2-008","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Texas A&M Institute for Advancing Health through Agriculture","orderIn":0},{"agency":"USDA-ARS Germplasm Evaluation","grantId":"58-6046-3-005","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Texas A&M Advancing Discovery to Market","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Texas Department of Agriculture","grantId":"GSC2024075","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Protoplasts","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cyamopsis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Plant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Knockout Techniques","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierN