ffiliation":"Bavarian Center for Cancer Research (BZKF), Lighthouse Cellular Immunotherapies, 97080 Würzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Peng H","lastName":"Peng","firstName":"Haiyong","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0312-1337"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology and Microbiology, The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology, University of Florida, Jupiter, FL 33458, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Rader C","lastName":"Rader","firstName":"Christoph","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology and Microbiology, The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology, University of Florida, Jupiter, FL 33458, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8024-0060"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0312-1337"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4276696,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Antib Ther","title":"Antibody therapeutics","essn":"2516-4236","issn":"2516-4236","isoabbreviation":"Antib Ther","nlmid":"101730822"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"576-594","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains challenging to treat and often requires intensive chemotherapy. In contrast to other hematologic malignancies, the development of effective antibody-based and cellular immunotherapies for AML has been limited by the scarcity of suitable target antigens.<h4>Methods</h4>We applied a phage display-based whole-cell panning method in which Fab-phage were biotinylated and captured, followed by next-generation sequencing (NGS), bioinformatic, and statistical analyses. Target deconvolution was performed using a CRISPR-Cas9 knockout library, fluorescence-activated cell sorting of antigen-negative cells, and NGS-based gRNA analysis. Selected candidates were further evaluated using primary AML patient cells and chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-T cell assays.<h4>Results</h4>We identified 28 unique monoclonal antibodies that preferentially bound AML cell lines. CRISPR-Cas9-based target deconvolution enabled efficient identification of three cognate antigens. Selected lead candidates were validated by staining primary cells from AML patients and were engineered into CAR constructs. CAR-T cells targeting the identified antigens mediated efficient eradication of AML cell lines and primary AML cells.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This integrated antibody-based antigen discovery and validation approach may accelerate the development of monoclonal antibody- and CAR-based immunotherapies for AML and other indications.","affiliation":"Department of Life Sciences, Graduate School of Arts and Sciences, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, 153-8902, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA181258","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA174844","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA204484","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R21 CA263240","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R21 CA229961","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Phage display","Acute myeloid leukemia","Cancer Immunotherapy","Antibody Discovery","Car-t","Crispr-cas9 Library Screening"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/abt/tbag043"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628138"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628138?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-04","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42391025","source":"MED","pmid":"42391025","pmcid":"PMC13384063","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13384063"]},"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btag483","title":"An interpretable deep learning framework uncovers features governing CRISPR-Cas9 genome-editing efficiency.","authorString":"Bakhtiyari N, Masoudi-Sobhanzadeh Y, Farajnia S, Kumar S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bakhtiyari N","lastName":"Bakhtiyari","firstName":"Nasim","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Drug Applied Research Center, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences, Tabriz, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Masoudi-Sobhanzadeh Y","lastName":"Masoudi-Sobhanzadeh","firstName":"Yosef","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2472-0980"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Engineering, Istanbul Rumeli University, Istanbul, Turkey."},{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Medicine, Faculty of Advanced Medical Sciences, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences, Tabriz, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Farajnia S","lastName":"Farajnia","firstName":"Safar","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Drug Applied Research Center, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences, Tabriz, Iran."},{"affiliation":"Biotechnology Research Center, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences, Tabriz, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar S","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Sushant","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2294-3988"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Princess Margaret Cancer Centre, University Health Network, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Biophysics, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2294-3988"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2472-0980"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"42","journalIssueId":4225057,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bioinformatics","title":"Bioinformatics (Oxford, England)","essn":"1367-4811","issn":"1367-4803","isoabbreviation":"Bioinformatics","nlmid":"9808944"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"btag483","abstractText":"<h4>Motivation</h4>CRISPR-Cas9 genome-editing efficiency is strongly influenced by the sequence composition and positional context of single-guide RNAs (sgRNAs). Although numerous deep learning-based models have been developed to predict Cas9 efficiency from sgRNA sequences, most operate as black boxes, offering limited insight into the sequence determinants underlying Cas9 activity. In addition, previous studies often overlook how the positional context of sequence motifs within sgRNAs influences their effects on Cas9 binding or cleavage.<h4>Results</h4>We introduce DeepCC9, an interpretable machine learning framework that combines explicit sequence feature extraction with a residual block-based deep architecture to improve interpretability and identify composition- and position-based motifs governing Cas9 genome-editing efficiency. We applied this method to multiple Cas9 variant datasets, achieving superior predictive performance compared with existing methods while enabling direct interpretation of sequence motifs and their positional effects. Our analysis uncovered 74 sequence motifs enriched or depleted at specific positions within sgRNAs and strongly associated with Cas9 efficiency, providing mechanistic insight into sequence features that influence guide performance. Together, these results establish DeepCC9 as a generalizable and interpretable framework for modeling sequence-function relationships and advancing the understanding of the sequence determinants underlying CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing.<h4>Availability and implementation</h4>The authors have implemented their algorithm in the Python programming language (version 3.X), which is accessible using (https://zenodo.org/records/20073890).","affiliation":"Drug Applied Research Center, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences, Tabriz, Iran.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Drug Applied Research Center, Tabriz University of Medical Sciences","grantId":"7130","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Terry Fox Research Institute","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Princess Margaret Cancer Foundation, Canada Research Chair Program","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computational Biology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Deep Learning"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btag483"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13384063"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13384063?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42691137","source":"MED","pmid":"42691137","pmcid":"PMC13643804","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13643804"]},"doi":"10.1093/g3journal/jkag211","title":"Optimizing CRISPR/Cas9 mutagenesis in Drosophila dendritic arborization neurons to avoid cytotoxicity.","authorString":"Duarte VN, Najafi V, Thompson-Peer KL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Duarte VN","lastName":"Duarte","firstName":"Vinicius N","initials":"VN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0904-4427"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Developmental and Cell Biology, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Najafi V","lastName":"Najafi","firstName":"Viana","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Developmental and Cell Biology, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Thompson-Peer KL","lastName":"Thompson-Peer","firstName":"Katherine L","initials":"KL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4200-3870"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Developmental and Cell Biology, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States."},{"affiliation":"Center for the Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States."},{"affiliation":"Sue and Bill Gross Stem Cell Research Center, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0904-4427"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4200-3870"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4285647,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"G3 (Bethesda)","title":"G3 (Bethesda, Md.)","essn":"2160-1836","issn":"2160-1836","isoabbreviation":"G3 (Bethesda)","nlmid":"101566598"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"jkag211","abstractText":"Genetic perturbations are one of the great strengths of the model organism Drosophila melanogaster, with approaches such as classical mutagenesis and RNA interference enabling a wealth of biological discoveries. A more recent approach for altering gene expression is CRISPR/Cas9-based mutagenesis. As with any new tool, however, its use must be optimized. High expression of Cas9 has been shown to cause cytotoxicity in some cell types, including class IV dendritic arborization (da) neurons. In this study, we provide evidence that Cas9 expression causes cytotoxicity in class I da neurons, in addition to class IV da neurons, both of which are widely used to study neuronal development and regeneration. We then systematically evaluated available Cas9 transgenes designed to titrate Cas9 expression, called uCas9 transgenes. We show that the expression of these uCas9 transgenes results in little to no cytotoxicity in various classes of da neurons. Immunostaining revealed drastic reductions in Cas9 protein levels for da neurons expressing the uCas9(L) transgene. Lastly, we demonstrate that the uCas9(L) transgene effectively and specifically gene edits in both class I and class IV da neurons, lowering the expression of GFP-tagged proteins and producing loss-of-function morphological phenotypes when targeting endogenous loci. Thus, we refine the use of CRISPR mutagenesis in Drosophila da neurons through titration of Gal4/UAS-mediated Cas9 expression using existing uCas9 transgenes, a feasible and flexible approach that may be useful for other labs encountering Cas9 cytotoxicity in their own model systems.","affiliation":"Department of Developmental and Cell Biology, University of California, Irvine, CA 92697, United States.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NINDS NIH HHS","acronym":"NS","grantId":"R00 NS097627","orderIn":0},{"agency":"University of California, Irvine","grantId":"T32MH119049","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"S10 OD032327","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Optical Biology Core Facility of the Developmental Biology Center","orderIn":0},{"agency":"UCI School of Biological Sciences ODEI Postdoctoral Excellence","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Cancer Center Support","grantId":"CA-62203","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Vienna Drosophila Resource Center","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Hellman Family Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Cancer Center Support","grantId":"NIH-S10OD032327-01","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"P40 OD018537","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Rose Hills Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Bloomington Drosophila Stock Center","grantId":"NIH P40OD018537","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"P30 CA062203","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","grantId":"T32 MH119049","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neurons","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dendrites","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Drosophila melanogaster","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mutagenesis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transgenes"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cytotoxicity","Drosophila","Mutagenesis","Sensory neuron","Dendrites","DA neuron","Crispr","Cas9","Dendrotoxicity"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/g3journal/jkag211"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13643804"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13643804?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"41256599","reference":"bioRxiv. 2025 Oct 04:2025.10.03.680356. doi: 10.1101/2025.10.03.680356.","type":"Update of","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-08","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41510162","source":"MED","pmid":"41510162","pmcid":"PMC12775815","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12775815"]},"doi":"10.7150/thno.122238","title":"Physical stimuli-responsive CRISPR-Cas9 systems for spatiotemporally precise control of genome engineering.","authorString":"Pan J, Li B, Wang Y, Han Y, Liu G, Sun SK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan J","lastName":"Pan","firstName":"Jinbin","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Functional Imaging, Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Tianjin 300052, China."},{"affiliation":"Athinoula A. Martinos Center for Biomedical Imaging, Institute for Innovation in Imaging (i 3 ), Department of Radiology, Massachusetts General Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02129, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Li B","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Bingjie","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Functional Imaging, Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Tianjin 300052, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Yaqiong","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Zhongshan Hospital, Fudan University, Shanghai 200032, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Han Y","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Yating","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Tianjin Medical University, Tianjin 300070, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu G","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Guijun","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Functional Imaging, Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Tianjin 300052, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun SK","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Shao-Kai","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medical Imaging, Division of Medical Technology, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Functional Imaging, Tianjin Medical University, Tianjin 300203, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4089164,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Theranostics","title":"Theranostics","essn":"1838-7640","issn":"1838-7640","isoabbreviation":"Theranostics","nlmid":"101552395"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2984-3010","abstractText":"The Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats-associated protein 9 (CRISPR-Cas9) endonuclease system has revolutionized biology research by enabling precise, efficient, and versatile genome editing. However, achieving spatiotemporally controlled gene editing within specific organs, tissues, or cells remains a major challenge, as unregulated CRISPR-Cas9 activity can lead to severe off-target effects, hindering its clinical translation. To enhance the on-target precision and reduce the unwanted consequences of aberrant or premature CRISPR-Cas9 activation, various strategies have been developed to regulate its function at translational or post-translational stages using diverse external physicochemical stimuli. While chemical molecule-inducible CRISPR-Cas9 systems have demonstrated significant progress, most of them still suffer from inherent deficiencies, such as unsatisfactory spatiotemporal precision, irreversibility, pharmacokinetic dependence, internal disturbance, and safety concerns related to chemical inducers. By contrast, externally applied physical stimuli provide distinct advantages for triggering CRISPR-Cas9 activity, offering superior spatiotemporal precision, reversibility, and biocompatibility. These features significantly enhance the controllability, target specificity, and practical applicability of CRISPR-Cas9 systems across diverse biological settings. This review systematically explores recent advances in physical stimuli-responsive CRISPR-Cas9 platforms, detailing their design strategies, activation mechanisms, and proof-of-concept applications. Furthermore, we provide a comparative analysis of different stimulation strategies, highlighting their respective characteristics, current limitations, and future prospects. A discussion on the persistent bench-to-bedside gap is also included, aiming to guide future development toward clinically viable solutions.","affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Functional Imaging, Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Tianjin 300052, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Engineering","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifi