{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5360-1254"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5598-5239"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5982-7844"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7678-8146"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7953-0765"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3859-4073"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4394-7953"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5004-2802"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8080-7546"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9261-5234"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"22","journalIssueId":4170418,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Syst Biol","title":"Molecular systems biology","essn":"1744-4292","issn":"1744-4292","isoabbreviation":"Mol Syst Biol","nlmid":"101235389"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"599-619","abstractText":"Dictyostelids are a species-rich clade of cellular slime molds that are widely found in soils and have been studied for over a century. Due to a lack of genome editing methods, most molecular research in Dictyostelids has focused on only a single species, Dictyostelium discoideum, which has severely limited broad-scale comparative analyses. Here, we introduce the first CRISPR-Cas9 editing approach that is cloning-free, selection-free, highly efficient, and effective across Dictyostelid species that diverged millions of years ago. Depending on the CRISPR-Cas9 target site, our editing approach generates knock-out efficiencies of up to 90% and knock-in efficiencies of up to 50% without a selective marker. We show that mutants can be isolated as soon as one day post-transfection, vastly outpacing existing methods for generating knock-outs, fusion proteins, and expression reporters. Leveraging single-cell sorting and fluorescent microscopy, we could readily apply our CRISPR-Cas9 editing approach to phylogenetically distant Dictyostelid species, including those that have never been genome edited before. Our methods therefore open the door to performing broad-scale genetic interrogations across the Dictyostelids.","affiliation":"Developmental Biology Unit, European Molecular Biology Laboratory, Heidelberg, 69117, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"European Research Council","grantId":"101116560","orderIn":0},{"agency":"European Molecular Biology Laboratory","orderIn":0},{"agency":"iXcore-iXlife-iXblue foundation","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dictyosteliida","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dictyostelium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Knockout Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Knock-In Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Dictyostelium discoideum","Cell Sorting","Genome Editing","Dictyostelids","Crispr-cas9"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44320-025-00180-8"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13046832"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13046832?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","gca","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42226151","source":"MED","pmid":"42226151","pmcid":"PMC13440110","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13440110"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12917-026-05602-9","title":"CRISPR-Cas9-mediated construction of a Streptococcus agalactiae vaccine for tilapia and evaluation of its protective efficacy.","authorString":"Huang M, Li X, Pan T, Wu D, Li G, Wu W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang M","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Mianlong","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li X","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Xiufang","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Guangxi Vocational University of Agriculture, Nanning, 530007, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Pan T","lastName":"Pan","firstName":"Taian","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu D","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Donghai","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li G","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Gonghe","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu W","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Wende","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China. wwd0194@163.com."},{"affiliation":"Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region Veterinary Biological Products Engineering Research Center, Nanning, 530004, China. wwd0194@163.com."},{"affiliation":"Guangxi Key Laboratory of Animal Breeding and Disease Prevention and Control, Nanning, 530004, China. wwd0194@163.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"22","journalIssueId":4209842,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Vet Res","title":"BMC veterinary research","essn":"1746-6148","issn":"1746-6148","isoabbreviation":"BMC Vet Res","nlmid":"101249759"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"456","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Streptococcus agalactiae (GBS) causes severe tilapia streptococcosis with heavy aquaculture losses; existing vaccines have administration or efficacy limitations. This study used CRISPR-Cas9 to construct recombinant Escherichia coli DH5α-ORF4-GFP (targeting GBS scpB gene ORF4 fragment), optimized tilapia immersion immunization doses/frequencies, and evaluated the vaccine's protective efficacy, biosafety and regulatory effects via multi-dimensional assays.<h4>Results</h4>The optimal regimen was single immersion at 1.5 × 10<sup>4</sup> CFU/mL, with a maximum RPS of 73.13% and stable 65.79% in validation. Immunized tilapia showed elevated immune indices (161.40% higher platelets) and numerical increases in globulin, normal liver/kidney function, and improved oxidative stress resistance with no tissue damage. The vaccine did not alter intestinal microbial richness but modulated community structure, enriching beneficial taxa such as Alphaproteobacteria, suggesting a potential interaction between vaccination and gut microbiota that may contribute to enhanced host defense.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In conclusion, this study successfully developed an effective and safe genetically engineered vaccine against GBS in tilapia. The precise CRISPR-Cas9-mediated construction strategy and confirmed immune protective effect provide a novel technical approach for controlling this disease in aquaculture and offer important references for the development of related genetically engineered vaccines.","affiliation":"College of Animal Science and Technology, Guangxi University, Nanning, 530004, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Guangxi Science and Technology Program","grantId":"AA17204081-1","orderIn":0},{"agency":"the Fangchenggang Science and Technology Program","grantId":"AB22013007","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Tilapia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Streptococcus agalactiae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Streptococcal Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Fish Diseases","me