omedical Research Centre, 30 Guilford Street, London WC1N 1EH, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7756-7232"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"34","journalIssueId":4217753,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Ther Adv","title":"Molecular therapy. Advances","essn":"3117-387X","issn":"3117-387X","isoabbreviation":"Mol Ther Adv","nlmid":"9919257804006676"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"201816","abstractText":"Translating CRISPR-Cas9-based homology-directed repair (HDR) strategies into clinical application remains a major challenge due to limited standardization, concerns over safety, and efficacy issues. Here, we present a comprehensive and clinically compliant preclinical framework for the <i>ex vivo</i> correction of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS) using a CRISPR-Cas9-AAV6 platform targeting hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs). In this study, we established a clinical-compatible platform enabling large-scale manufacturing while preserving HSPC viability, stemness, and multilineage functionality. To overcome low HSPC long-term engraftment, we fine-tuned AAV dosing and transiently modulated p53BP-dependent DNA damage response pathway, achieving significantly improved <i>in vivo</i> correction and repopulation. Importantly, we implemented a multi-tiered genotoxicity assessment strategy, integrating in silico, genome-wide, and orthogonal assays, revealing a largely favorable safety profile with minimal off-target risks and no evidence of clonal dominance or transformation during the study period. Longitudinal <i>in vivo</i> safety monitoring revealed donor-specific rare off-target events and structural variants. This highlights the crucial importance of patient monitoring after transplantation, further emphasized by the identification of a <i>de novo</i> chromosomal rearrangement that could be detected exclusively following cell engraftment in mice. This work offers a robust and adaptable roadmap for future HDR-based gene editing platforms, establishing critical benchmarks for efficacy, safety, and regulatory readiness in the development of advanced therapeutic medicinal products.","affiliation":"Infection, Immunity & Inflammation Research and Teaching Department, UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health, University College London, 20 Guilford Street, London WC1N 1DZ, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"UKRI Medical Research Council","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Wellcome Trust","acronym":"WT","grantId":"217112/Z/19/Z","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ministero dell&apos;Istruzione dell&apos;Università e della Ricerca","grantId":"FIS-2023-00171","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Great Ormond Street Hospital Children&apos;s Charity","grantId":"V4522","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Great Ormond Street Hospital Children&apos;s Charity","grantId":"VS0420","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Jeffrey Modell Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Preclinical","Gene Editing","Homology-directed Repair","Hematopoietic Stem And Progenitor Cells"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.omta.2026.201816"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476499"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476499?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41846089","source":"MED","pmid":"41846089","pmcid":"PMC13011053","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13011053"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.psj.2026.106722","title":"High genotoxicity of CRISPR/Cas9 versus limited efficacy of CRISPRi in chicken primordial germ cells.","authorString":"Zhang C, Wang M, He B, Yang X, Li XZ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang C","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Chen","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for RNA Medicine, The Fourth Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, International Medical School, International Institutes of Medicine, Yiwu, Zhejiang, 322000, PR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang M","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Mengqi","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Life Science, Henan Normal University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453007, PR China."}]}},{"fullName":"He B","lastName":"He","firstName":"Banghua","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Life Science, Henan Normal University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453007, PR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang X","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Xianguang","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Life Science, Henan Normal University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453007, PR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li XZ","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Xin Zhiguo","initials":"XZ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for RNA Medicine, The Fourth Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, International Medical School, International Institutes of Medicine, Yiwu, Zhejiang, 322000, PR China.. Electronic address: xinlirna@zju.edu.cn."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9625-507X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"105","journalIssueId":4202915,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Poult Sci","title":"Poultry science","essn":"1525-3171","issn":"0032-5791","isoabbreviation":"Poult Sci","nlmid":"0401150"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"106722","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9 technology has transformed genome editing across species; however, its application in avian germ cells remains constrained-not only by editing efficiency, but also by limited evaluation of potential genotoxic effects. In this study, we systematically assessed the performance and genomic safety of CRISPR/Cas9 and CRISPR interference (CRISPRi) in chicken primordial germ cells (PGCs). While CRISPR/Cas9 achieved high editing efficiency, it simultaneously induced substantial DNA damage, apoptosis, and sex-specific cell cycle arrest, revealing the pronounced genotoxic sensitivity of PGCs. In contrast, CRISPRi was well tolerated but failed to achieve effective gene repression in chicken cells. Comparative experiments showed that CRISPRi functioned efficiently in human 293T cells but not in chicken PGCs or somatic DF-1 cells, suggesting species-dependent limitations of mammalian-optimized repression systems. Together, these findings reveal a fundamental trade-off-\"efficient but toxic\" versus \"safe but ineffective\"-when applying CRISPR tools to avian germ cells. Our results highlight the need for species-adapted, low-toxicity genome-editing platforms in poultry and provide a framework for evaluating editing strategies in developmentally sensitive cell types.","affiliation":"Center for RNA Medicine, The Fourth Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, International Medical School, International Institutes of Medicine, Yiwu, Zhejiang, 322000, PR China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Zhejiang University","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Germ Cells"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Chickens","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"DNA Damage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Chicken","Genotoxicity","Primordial germ cell","Crispr Interference","Crispr/cas9"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psj.2026.106722"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13011053"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13011053?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42584708","source":"MED","pmid":"42584708","pmcid":"PMC13469492","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13469492"]},"doi":"10.1007/s00439-026-02863-0","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-based repair of a heterozygous HNF1A mutation in patient-derived hiPSCs.","authorString":"Skoczek D, Hohendorff J, Malecki MT, Roig-Merino A, Bak RO, Kachamakova-Trojanowska N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Skoczek D","lastName":"Skoczek","firstName":"Dawid","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Malopolska Centre of Biotechnology, Jagiellonian University, Gronostajowa str 7a, 30-387, Krakow, Poland."},{"affiliation":"Doctoral School of Exact and Natural Sciences, Jagiellonian University, Lojasiewicza str 11, 30-387, Krakow, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Hohendorff J","lastName":"Hohendorff","firstName":"Jerzy","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Metabolic Diseases, Jagiellonian University Medical College, Jakubowskiego str 2, 30-688, Krakow, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Malecki MT","lastName":"Malecki","firstName":"Maciej T","initials":"MT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Metabolic Diseases, Jagiellonian University Medical College, Jakubowskiego str 2, 30-688, Krakow, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Roig-Merino A","lastName":"Roig-Merino","firstName":"Alicia","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"MaxCyte Inc, Rockville, MD, 20850, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bak RO","lastName":"Bak","firstName":"Rasmus O","initials":"RO","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7383-0297"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedicine, Aarhus University, Aarhus, 8000, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Kachamakova-Trojanowska N","lastName":"Kachamakova-Trojanowska","firstName":"Neli","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3226-0726"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Malopolska Centre of Biotechnology, Jagiellonian University, Gronostajowa str 7a, 30-387, Krakow, Poland. neli.kachamakova-trojanowska@uj.edu.pl."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3226-0726"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7383-0297"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"145","journalIssueId":4240452,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Hum Genet","title":"Human genetics","essn":"1432-1203","issn":"0340-6717","isoabbreviation":"Hum Genet","nlmid":"7613873"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"69","abstractText":"Human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) represent a powerful platform for disease modeling, especially in monogenic diseases as they preserve the donor's genetic background while enabling directed differentiation into disease-relevant cell types. This makes them highly suitable for studying disease mechanisms in a patient-specific and physiologically relevant context. Although CRISPR/Cas9 is widely applied for genome editing, precise correction of pathogenic variants in hiPSCs remains challenging due to the lack of standardized CRISPR component selection and experimental design. Here, we describe an optimized CRISPR-based strategy for correcting a heterozygous HNF1A frameshift mutation (c.235_236insG; p.Glu79Glyfs*16) in HNF1A-MODY patient-derived hiPSCs. Using electroporation, we efficiently delivered CRISPR components, including a ribonucleoprotein complex of Cas9 and single-guide RNA, along with a single-stranded oligodeoxynucleotide repair template. Corrected hiPSC lines were validated for pluripotency, absence of exogenous reprogramming factors, and off-target effects. Additionally, we discuss key technical challenges encountered during the editing process and provide practical recommendations that may improve the generation of mutation-corrected hiPSC lines. These guidelines could serve as a useful reference for researchers employing CRISPR-based strategies for generation of reliable disease modelling tools.","affiliation":"Malopolska Centre of Biotechnology, Jagiellonian University, Gronostajowa str 7a, 30-387, Krakow, Poland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Narodowe Centrum Nauki","grantId":"2020/38/E/NZ3/00516","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Exons"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hepatocyte Nuclear Factor 1-alpha","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Induced Pluripotent Stem Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TR","qualifierName":"transplantation"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Recombinational DNA Repair"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Hepatocyte Nuclear Factor 1-alpha","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"HNF1A protein, human","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00439-026-02863-0"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13469492"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13469492?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["arrayexpress","omim","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41953815","source":"MED","pmid":"41953815","pmcid":"PMC13055883","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13055883"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpls.2026.1754287","title":"Genome editing and regeneration pipeline for engineering disease resistance in tomato using CRISPR/Cas9.","authorString":"Erdoğan İ, Debbarma R, Sherry M, Mancak İ, Grant M, Tör M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Erdoğan İ","lastName":"Erdoğan","firstName":"İbrahim","initials":"İ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Warwick, School of Life Sciences, Elizabeth Creak Horticultural Technology Centre (ECHTC), Coventry, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Debbarma R","lastName":"Debbarma","firstName":"Ruma","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Warwick, School of Life Sciences, Elizabeth Creak Horticultural Technology Centre (ECHTC), Coventry, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Sherry M","lastName":"Sherry","firstName":"Minu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Warwick, School of Life Sciences, Elizabeth Creak Horticultural Technology Centre (ECHTC), Coventry, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Mancak İ","lastName":"Mancak","firstName":"İbrahim","initials":"İ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Manier Seeds, Adana, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Grant M","lastName":"Grant","firstName":"Murray","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Warwick, School of Life Sciences, Elizabeth Creak Horticultural Technology Centre (ECHTC), Coventry, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Tör M","lastName":"Tör","firstName":"Mahmut","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Plant and Microbial Biosciences Research Unit, School of Science and the Environment, University of Worcester, Worcester, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112080,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Plant Sci","title":"Frontiers in plant science","essn":"1664-462X","issn":"1664-462X","isoabbreviation":"Front Plant Sci","nlmid":"101568200"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1754287","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9 (Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats/Cas9)-based genome editing has emerged as a powerful tool for developing disease-resistant crops. Here, we present a comprehensive and reproducible protocol for applying CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in tomato (<i>Solanum lycopersicum</i>), covering guide RNA (gRNA) design using CRISPOR, Golden Gate vector assembly, <i>Agrobacterium</i>-mediated transformation, plant regeneration, and molecular validation of edited plants. The workflow integrates standardized bioinformatics and sequencing-based validation tools, including DSDecodeM, TIDE, and protein-level impact analysis, to confirm targeted mutations and assess editing efficiency. Quantitative benchmarks for regeneration, transformation, and editing efficiencies were provided to support reproducibility. This protocol offers an integrated pipeline for generating and validating targeted gene knockouts in tomatoes and is intended to facilitate functional genomic studies and the development of disease-resistant cultivars. However, it is more widely applicable to gene editing in tomato plants.","affiliation":"University of Warwick, School of Life Sciences, Elizabeth Creak Horticultural Technology Centre (ECHTC), Coventry, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["methods-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Disease resistance","Susceptibility gene","Crop Improvement","Genome Editing","Targeted Mutations"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1754287"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13055883"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13055883?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42494967","source":"MED","pmid":"42494967","pmcid":"PMC13393434","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13393434"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.abiote.2026.100075","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-mediated knockout of &lt;i&gt;MsMLO1&lt;/i&gt; confers enhanced powdery mildew resistance in alfalfa.","authorString":"Hou Z, Li Q, Wang Y, Zong H, Zhao W, Dong Y, Liu H, Wu Q, Chen J, Wang W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hou Z","lastName":"Hou","firstName":"Zhuoting","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Science and Technology, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, 710129, China."},{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li Q","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Qi","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Science and Technology, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, 710129, China."},{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Yongqiang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zong H","lastName":"Zong","firstName":"Hang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Science and Technology, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, 710129, China."},{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao W","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Weiyue","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CAS Key Laboratory of Tropical Plant Resources and Sustainable Use, CAS Center for Excellence in Molecular Plant Sciences, Xishuangbanna Tropical Botanical Garden, Kunming, 650223, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dong Y","lastName":"Dong","firstName":"Yiling","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu H","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Huanli","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanjie Institute of Forage, Yangling, 712100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu Q","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Qing","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CAS Key Laboratory of Tropical Plant Resources and Sustainable Use, CAS Center for Excellence in Molecular Plant Sciences, Xishuangbanna Tropical Botanical Garden, Kunming, 650223, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen J","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Jianghua","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CAS Key Laboratory of Tropical Plant Resources and Sustainable Use, CAS Center for Excellence in Molecular Plant Sciences, Xishuangbanna Tropical Botanical Garden, Kunming, 650223, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang W","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Wen","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Science and Technology, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, 710129, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4231474,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"aBIOTECH","title":"aBIOTECH","essn":"2662-1738","issn":"2096-6326","isoabbreviation":"aBIOTECH","nlmid":"9918231191406676"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100075","abstractText":"Powdery mildew, a disease primarily caused by <i>Erysiphe pisi</i>, is one of the most devastating foliar diseases of alfalfa (<i>Medicago sativa</i> L.), a globally important forage crop. The <i>MLO</i> gene is a well-known susceptibility factor for powdery mildew in many plant species, but its role in alfalfa remains unclear. In this study, we used the CRISPR/Cas9 system to edit <i>MsMLO1</i> in alfalfa cultivar 'Aohan'. We designed an sgRNA based on a conserved target site in the exon region of this gene, and introduced the genome-editing vector into alfalfa via <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i>-mediated transformation. Among the regenerated plants, we obtained a homozygous mutant (<i>Msmlo1</i>-3) with all four haplotypes edited, as well as two heterozygous mutants (<i>Msmlo1-</i>1 and <i>Msmlo1</i>-2). In disease-resistance assays, the homozygous mutant exhibited significantly enhanced resistance to <i>E. pisi</i>, with markedly reduced hyphal growth and conidial density compared to wild-type (WT) plants, whereas heterozygous mutants displayed no obvious resistance. Importantly, the <i>Msmlo1</i>-3 mutant showed no detectable growth penalties or altered agricultural traits grown in a greenhouse for six months. Transcriptome analysis revealed that the <i>MsMLO1</i> mutation affects a broad gene regulatory network, including genes encoding transcription factors (<i>MYB</i>, <i>AP2/ERF</i>, <i>WRKY</i>) and genes involved in phyto hormone signaling, cell wall remodeling, secondary metabolism, and reactive oxygen species pathways. Therefore CRISPR/Cas9-mediated knockout of <i>MsMLO1</i> conferred robust and durable powdery mildew resistance without compromising normal plant growth, highlighting the potential of <i>MsMLO1</i> as a target for breeding disease-resistant in alfalfa.","affiliation":"School of Life Science and Technology, Northwestern Polytechnical Uni